**Card carousel motion (Pain + Promise, shared `startOnbCardScroll`).** The stack translates up one card per step over ~1067ms `ease-in-out`, pauses 600ms, then recycles the top card to the bottom. Per step the leaving card (index 0) fades to 0 and scales to 0.9 while the arriving card (index 3) fades to 1 and scales up from 0.9 — both driven by `onb1CardSlot`, so the resting state of index 3 must equal the end state of index 0 or the recycled card pops in at full size. No overshoot easing anywhere in the loop; the old `cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.4, 0.64, 1)` bounce was removed on request and `tests/selfcheck.js` fails if a >1 control point comes back. `#newUserWrongUrl` shares the same loop.

**Modal entry motion (Pain/Promise only).** On every landing (initial open + each `goTo(0|1)`), `playOnbModalEnter` runs — lines rise, chip lands, CTA slides in. **Pain→Promise forward:** during crossfade Promise gets `is-modal-shell-only` (white modal only, no copy); after fade, `playOnbModalEnter` on Promise. Backward Promise→Pain still uses `paintOnbCopySlots` morph.

**Calendar permissions (normal login).** Promise **Next** → `openPromisePermsSheet()` (not `goTo(2)`). Sheet `#onbPermsSheet` in `#onbPermsOverlay` on `#onboardingPromise`. Allow → `startConnectingAndLogin()` with perms sheet still open; auth slides on top.

**Do not:** revert to `goTo(2)` for Promise Next; close perms before Connecting; skip `is-modal-shell-only` on Pain→Promise (causes copy flash before entry anim).

**Copy + button morph (`paintOnbCopySlots`, Pain↔Promise only).** Phases mirror the card loop: `enter` (parked), `settle`, `exit`, `rest`. The heading translates ±24px — out to the left and in from the right going forward, mirrored on Back — and takes its opacity from the section crossfade, because fading it again makes it vanish before it lands. The button block morphs between one full-width primary and Back+Next by animating the secondary's `max-width` 0 ↔ its natural half plus the row's `column-gap` 0 ↔ 12; flex hands the reclaimed space to the primary, so one property shrinks one button and grows the other. **Both screens animate to the same geometry** (`enter` and `exit` share the collapsed layout) — morphing only the incoming screen leaves the outgoing label sitting beside it as a doubled "Next" for the whole crossfade. The secondary opens to `calc(50% - var(--space-3) / 2)`, not a round 50%, so the two primaries stay pixel-identical instead of drifting ~6px apart. `.onb-btn` carries `overflow: hidden` and `.onb-btn-inner` `white-space: nowrap` so the squeezed label clips instead of reflowing.

**Ticker dots.** Each screen owns its own `.onb-ticker`, and the markup pre-sets each one to its own step — so `goTo` rewinds the incoming ticker to the outgoing step instantly (`setOnbTickerActive(..., { instant: true })`), then animates **both** tickers to the new step during the crossfade. The dots transition `width` 6↔28 and `background-color` over 0.5s ease-in-out in CSS.

