## Supabase (project `ayzqfwtoeckgycmqzlve`)

- Tables: `users` (one-to-one profile for `auth.users`), `videohistory` (scheduled video lifecycle; multi-session cols `session_group_id`, `session_index`, `session_count`, `video_offset_start_sec`, `video_offset_end_sec`, `all_sessions_watched`), `feedback`, `button_clicks` (append-only interaction analytics), `referral_codes` (one per user), `referral_redemptions` (one per redeemed user; `notified_at` timestamptz is the idempotency key that stops a replayed `sendreferralnotification` call from mailbombing the referrer), `user_slot_preferences` (canonical selected days/times + setup snapshot), `calendar_slot_scores` (latest 7×7 score grid), `calendar_scan_runs` (scan audit/backoff), `slot_algorithm_config` (server-managed thresholds; includes `LONG_VIDEO_THRESHOLD_MINUTES` + `QUEUE_*` intercept keys), `queue_intercept_state` (per-user intercept cooldown: `last_shown_at`, `last_shown_after_weeks`).
- Removed in migration `consolidate_and_harden_public_schema`: `UserTokens` (write-only stale base64 token copies; security/revocation risk), `UserSlots` (redundant dual-write), and unused `videohistory.notified` / `published_at`. Three legacy-only `UserSlots` rows belonged to deleted auth users and were intentionally discarded; all 10 active canonical preference rows were preserved.
- Full logout wipe (`remember` unchecked): `wipeUserRemoteData` deletes `referral_redemptions`, `referral_codes`, `videohistory`, `user_slot_preferences`, `calendar_slot_scores`, `calendar_scan_runs`, `feedback`, `queue_intercept_state`, then `users`. `button_clicks` cascade when `users` is deleted. Migration `logout_wipe_delete_policies` added the missing DELETE RLS/grants.
- Edge Functions (deployed & live): `sendWelcomeEmail`, `sendreferralnotification` (kept deployed and hardened, but **no longer invoked** — the popup's referral UI was removed). Both authenticate with `auth.getUser()` and return `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` only for `chrome-extension://hkekbdlgnmjpbaijipkanenaeabhegfk`. `scanAndScore` exists in repo only, not deployed.
- **Retired 2026-08-05** (bodies replaced with a `410 Gone` stub, `verify_jwt` on; delete the slugs in the dashboard to remove the routes entirely): `exchange-token`, `send-reminder-email`, `sendDailyEmails`, `sendReferralNotification` (PascalCase twin). See the security-pass entry under History of fixes for what each one exposed. The live daily send is `scripts/sendDailyEmails.ts` via GitHub Actions, unaffected.
- **`verify_jwt: true` is not authentication.** It only proves the caller presented a JWT signed by this project — and the anon key shipped inside the extension is exactly that. Every function must call `auth.getUser()` (or check a shared secret) itself. `sendDailyEmails` had no in-function check, so the public anon key was enough to mass-mail every user.
- Every public table has RLS. Policies are explicitly `TO authenticated`, use `(select auth.uid())`, and have one policy per operation. API grants are least-privilege; `anon` has no public-table access. Client profile reads are column-limited to `id`, `name`, and `referral_popup_shown`; email/avatar are not broadly readable.
- Reporting views `button_result`, `history_results`, and `referral_summary` remain `security_invoker` dashboard/service-role helpers and are revoked from extension roles.
- Active foreign keys cascade user deletion; query-shaped indexes cover history, scan, score, feedback, and click access. Unique constraints enforce one referral code and one redemption per user.
- Auth: Google OIDC via `signInWithIdToken`; JWT lives 1 h; refresh tokens are single-use with reuse detection.

**Security advisors: 0 errors, 2 warnings — both platform settings, neither fixable by SQL/migration or MCP.** Don't waste a session hunting for a code fix.

| Warning | Why it's open | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `auth_leaked_password_protection` | Dashboard/Management-API toggle only. **Functionally moot here** — the project is Google OIDC exclusively, there is no email/password sign-in, so there are no passwords to check against HaveIBeenPwned. Requires Pro plan. | Dashboard → Authentication → Sign In / Providers → Email → enable "Prevent use of leaked passwords" |
| `vulnerable_postgres_version` | Running `supabase-postgres-15.8.1.054`; patches ship as a platform upgrade, which takes brief downtime. | Dashboard → Settings → Infrastructure → Upgrade project. Take a backup first; the extension will 5xx for a few minutes |

### Slot algorithm v1

- **Scores ≠ prefs.** freeBusy scan writes `calendar_slot_scores` only. Suggestions fill `selected_*` **once** (empty prefs). Rescan / reconnect / opportunistic never overwrite user selection — **except** explicit **`triggeredBy: 'prefs-banner'`** from the prefs scan banner (user clicked **Click here**).
- **Live path:** popup `analyzeAndSavePrefs` + `lib/slot-algorithm.js` (client freeBusy). Trigger A: `openSchedPrefs` rescans if newest `scanned_at` > `STALENESS_DAYS` — **not awaited**, the sheet opens immediately. Trigger B (server cron) deferred — no Google refresh token server-side yet.
- **Resume:** after onboarding scan, `resumeDestination` → Schedule if days+times set; else day/time prefs sheet. Wrong-URL first login still lands on `#wrongUrlFallAnim`.
- **Bucket keys** stay existing `SLOT_RANGES` labels (`Morning (6–9)`, … `Late Night (12–3)`), not plan-draft `6-9am` strings. Late Night = 00:00–03:00 of that same calendar day, matching the scheduler.
- **Scoring:** per (weekday, bucket) cell, `score` = share of sampled days clearing `FREE_RATIO_THRESHOLD`; `avgFreeRatio` keeps the continuous mean so an all-zero grid (moderately busy calendar) can still be ranked. Days/buckets rank on the **mean** across the other axis — max made every day with one free night look identical.
- **Failure handling:** `fetchFreeBusyRange` **throws** on non-2xx, on per-calendar `errors` inside a 200, and on failed 401 re-auth (one retry max). An empty busy array must only ever mean "genuinely free". Degraded per-month fallback scores **only the months that answered** (`coveredRanges`); unfetched months are never counted as free.
- **Scan locking:** `scanIsBlocked` — `running` rows expire after `SCAN_LOCK_TTL_MS` (10 min) because a popup closed mid-scan never writes `completed_at`; `not_before` backoff applies to `opportunistic` rescans only, not user-triggered ones.
- **Checks:** `node tests/slot-algorithm-selfcheck.js` (behaviour) and `node tests/slot-algorithm-stress.js` (jam-packed / free / hostile input / DST / 26k events / clamp guard, with timings).

### scanAndScore Edge Function (written, NOT deployed)

`supabase/functions/scanAndScore/` — `index.ts` + `tz.mjs`. Correct and reviewed, but deliberately undeployed.

- **Why undeployed:** it takes `googleAccessToken` from the request body, so it can only run while the popup is open holding a live token — at which point the client already scans in 1–60 ms. It only earns its keep once a server-held Google **refresh** token exists (Trigger B / cron). Deploying it as-is buys a network round trip and a second copy of the algorithm.
- **Contract:** `POST { googleAccessToken, timeZone, triggeredBy? }` + Bearer Supabase JWT. Writes `calendar_slot_scores` only — never `user_slot_preferences`, so a server rescan can't overwrite a user's selection.
- **Timezone is mandatory and validated.** Edge Functions run in UTC; buckets are wall-clock ranges in the user's zone. `tz.mjs` (`zonedToUtcMs` / `civilNow`) resolves each boundary via `Intl`, re-reading the offset at the candidate instant so DST transitions are exact (spring-forward Late Night = 2 h, fall-back = 4 h). `isValidTimeZone` rejects `''`/non-strings because `Intl` silently falls back to the host zone for those — which would score everyone against UTC.
- **Parity with the client:** same `SLOT_RANGES` keys, same `FREE_RATIO_THRESHOLD` / `MIN_SAMPLE_SIZE` / `ALGORITHM_VERSION` from `slot_algorithm_config`, same 10-min scan-lock TTL and 1-hour failure backoff, same "throw rather than treat an empty busy array as free" rule. `lib/slot-algorithm.js` stays the reference implementation — change it first, mirror here.
- Scoring uses one globally merged, window-clamped interval list plus binary search per bucket, instead of the client's per-day index; simpler and DST-safe. It writes no `avgFreeRatio` because it produces scores only, not suggestions.
- **Check:** `node tests/scanandscore-tz-selfcheck.mjs`.

## Auth flow (the load-bearing part)

1. Login: **silent** OAuth → background (`login` + `silent: true`); **interactive** OAuth → popup (`launchWebAuthFlow` + user gesture) → `completeLogin` → `auth.signInWithIdToken` → session saved only to `chrome.storage.local` (`supabase_token`, `supabase_refresh`, `google_access_token`, `userId`) → upsert `users` → fire `sendWelcomeEmail`. `auth_oauth_lock` serializes all `launchWebAuthFlow` calls across popup + background. There is no server-side token-copy table.
2. Post-login scan: decorative loop (`startOnbCalScan`) + `analyzeAndSavePrefs` inside **`finishPostAuthScan`**. **Watch URL:** auth down → schedule skeleton → `#onbAnalyzeOverlay` on `#popupWrapper` → scan → sheet down → `initPopup()`. **Wrong URL first login:** `#onboardingAnalyzing` mock chrome + `goTo(3)`. On success: `new-wrong-url` → `#wrongUrlFallAnim`; else → `initPopup()` (+ prefs resume if incomplete).
3. Stay logged in: 50-min alarm refreshes the Supabase session and silently renews the Google token (`prompt=none`). Popup open runs `auth.setSession(stored pair)`. Every refresh persists the rotated pair back to storage.
4. Usage: Calendar API (freeBusy, event create) uses the Google token — on 401, silent re-auth then one retry; DB reads/writes use the Supabase JWT. Slot finder reads only canonical `user_slot_preferences`.

